00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:50.210
And there was a delicious nugget for this week.

704
00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:51.750
A quote of the day.

705
00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:53.070
Quote of the day.

706
00:27:53.590 --> 00:28:01.270
On Thursday, December 13th, 2012, I had an exchange with Oscar Nunez, and I emailed you about it, Jenna.

707
00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:01.830
Okay.

708
00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.110
I wrote the following.

709
00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:07.090
So, we are in hair and makeup right now.

710
00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.350
We are talking about movies, et cetera.

711
00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:13.030
Oscar recently saw Finding a Friend for the End of the World.

712
00:28:13.450 --> 00:28:16.690
He said he liked the movie, and then he sighed and said,

713
00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:22.650
Steve, man, he gets to make out with Keira Knightley and Anne Hathaway.

714
00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:24.910
I get f***ing Jack Coleman'd.

715
00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:27.270
And we all burst out laughing.

716
00:28:29.650 --> 00:28:31.630
And you wrote back, ha, ha, ha.

717
00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:35.300
Oh, my goodness.

718
00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:37.580
Oh, that is very funny.

719
00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.140
That's my digital clutter quote of the day.

720
00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:43.540
Well, let's get back to the episode.

721
00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:44.160
Let's begin.

722
00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:47.460
We're going to start with a Pam talking head.

723
00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:52.600
She says she's putting in a lot of hours on this mural, and her boss is okay with it, because guess what?

724
00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:53.680
He's in the Bahamas.

725
00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:57.020
Yes, this was a talking head on the move.

726
00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.000
A lot went into this.

727
00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:00.680
I remember setting up this scene.

728
00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:05.200
There was originally this scene where I was in the parking lot, and I was talking on the phone to Jim.

729
00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:06.720
Yeah, it was in deleted scenes.

730
00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:11.380
It was kind of like a flirty conversation where Jim's like, you come here this weekend, and you're like, I'm busy.

731
00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:12.560
You come here.

732
00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:13.260
Like, yes.

733
00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:13.780
Like that.

734
00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:15.340
It was like, who's going to visit who?

735
00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:15.820
Right.

736
00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:19.240
So then we were going to cut to Pam doing this talking head in the warehouse.

737
00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:27.960
And it was originally supposed to be Pam standing kind of with her back to the mural, talking about how excited she was to work on it.

738
00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:31.620
And then the camera would pan up, and we would see the graffiti, and Pam would freak out.

739
00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:32.300
Okay.

740
00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:42.600
The problem with that was, when we got in place, we realized Pam would have seen the vandalism as soon as she walked into the warehouse.

741
00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:43.000
Yeah.

742
00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:44.000
How could you miss it?

743
00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:44.960
How could she miss it?

744
00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:56.100
So, Lee said, why don't we do this talking head on the move so that when she gets to the end of her talking head, she's just coming around the corner of the warehouse shelves,

745
00:29:56.100 --> 00:30:00.080
and then she sees the vandalism, and the camera swings up to see it, too.

746
00:30:00.080 --> 00:30:02.260
And we were like, yes, that's great.

747
00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:06.780
But timing it out exactly perfectly was a little tricky.

748
00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:14.920
So what we did was, I stood in the spot where I needed to be to realize it, and then I said my lines, and we backed up, and then that was our starting position.

749
00:30:15.340 --> 00:30:15.740
Oh.

750
00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:22.740
I don't know why I remember that so vividly, but I think it's just because it's the first time that, like, me and a whole camera crew went backwards.

751
00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:23.180
Yeah.

752
00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:26.060
Well, let's talk about what was on the mural.

753
00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:29.240
It's a whole bunch of big old butts.

754
00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:30.200
Yeah.

755
00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:30.920
Mm-hmm.

756
00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:32.740
And the words, this sucks.

757
00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:33.840
Oh, yeah, there's that.

758
00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:34.940
It's so mean.

759
00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:36.700
It's really aggressive.

760
00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:39.820
We had a fan question from Bailey H.

761
00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:41.880
in Overland Park, Kansas, who said,

762
00:30:42.260 --> 00:30:46.860
Was the shape painted on Pam's mural always scripted to be butts?

763
00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:54.500
I feel like a different shape is more common in graffiti, but maybe it wouldn't pass standards and practices without a whole lot of blurs.

764
00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:55.040
Uh-huh.

765
00:30:55.720 --> 00:31:00.140
So, Bailey, this scene really evolved through many drafts.

766
00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:04.080
In the table redraft, the graffiti was described like this.

767
00:31:04.740 --> 00:31:05.420
Quote,

768
00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:14.460
The mural is now covered in splashes of red paint, crude drawings, and a few messages like, this sucks, and HIDE rules.

769
00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:15.240
Mm-hmm.

770
00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:15.740
Mm.

771
00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:21.940
And then when Pam entered, Val was going to apologize to her and say, I'm just seeing this myself.

772
00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:24.760
I think the guys got drunk last night and did it.

773
00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:26.120
Okay.

774
00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:27.700
So that was like the original moment.

775
00:31:27.700 --> 00:31:31.780
Then, in the original shooting draft, it was described like this.

776
00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:32.820
Quote,

777
00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:36.760
The mural now has some obvious striking graffiti on it.

778
00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:41.900
There are a couple of crudely drawn butts, and someone has written this sucks.

779
00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:44.560
And then they took out that extra part with Val.

780
00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:48.260
And, Bailey, standards and practices did have notes on this.

781
00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:49.160
I bet.

782
00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:50.580
Here is what they said.

783
00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:51.560
They said,

784
00:31:52.040 --> 00:31:56.320
The crudely painted butts on the wall should not be lifelike in appearance.

785
00:31:58.980 --> 00:31:59.460
Lifelike.

786
00:31:59.620 --> 00:31:59.780
Yes.

787
00:31:59.920 --> 00:32:00.980
Very cartoony.

788
00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:02.300
Can't be too realistic.

789
00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:03.000
Exactly.

790
00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:09.520
Well, you know, I asked our writer, Owen, was it always the plan for Pam's mural to be vandalized?

791
00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:11.160
And here's what he had to say.

792
00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:13.980
He said he didn't think it was always the plan.

793
00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:18.200
It started as a way for Pam to flex her art muscles at work.

794
00:32:18.240 --> 00:32:21.380
But then when they started the storyline with Brian the Boom Guy,

795
00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:26.620
they wanted to create an intense situation for Pam that he could help her with.

796
00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:29.560
So, the fight over the mural fit right in.

797
00:32:30.340 --> 00:32:30.580
Yes.

798
00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:31.660
You know, and it's interesting.

799
00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:36.460
I remember being in these discussions because, you know, a big question when we decided on

800
00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:38.440
this storyline was, well, who did it?

801
00:32:38.700 --> 00:32:39.020
Right.

802
00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:40.900
Because whoever did it is going to get fired.

803
00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:48.400
And we kind of thought, like, oh, Hide had been giving Pam grief when she was painting.

804
00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:49.540
Maybe it's him.

805
00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:55.100
Wait, can I say in defense of Hide, not so much grief as just, like, hey, get started.

806
00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:56.580
He had, like, had critiques.

807
00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:57.520
He had thoughts.

808
00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:00.780
But it wasn't so much like he was not a fan of what she was doing.

809
00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:03.080
He just had a lot of opinions about it.

810
00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:03.500
Yes.

811
00:33:03.840 --> 00:33:05.260
But we didn't want to fire Hide.

812
00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:06.260
Of course not.

813
00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:11.720
So, we did that kind of sneaky sneak thing where we're like, I guess we'll reveal a new

814
00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:14.540
warehouse worker who will be responsible for this.

815
00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:16.500
That's how they came up with the character of Frank.

816
00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:22.280
But another thing was that later we knew that Frank and Brian were going to fight it out.

817
00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:22.700
Yeah.

818
00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:28.120
And, you know, Brian the boom guy was, like, a kind of big, muscly guy.

819
00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:31.060
And we didn't want him to beat up Hide.

820
00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:34.660
That didn't seem – also, Hide didn't seem like a person who would, like, throw punches

821
00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:35.440
over this, right?

822
00:33:35.440 --> 00:33:37.120
No, Hide's not going to charge Pam.

823
00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:37.480
No.

824
00:33:37.600 --> 00:33:39.040
I think Hide actually likes Pam.

825
00:33:39.200 --> 00:33:41.660
He just has opinions about her art, like I said.

826
00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:47.740
But I totally see in casting that you needed someone big who looked intimidating if they

827
00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:48.540
were going to run at you.

828
00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:49.180
Exactly.

829
00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:50.640
I mean, we'll get to it.

830
00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:51.800
We're a little ahead of ourselves.

831
00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:52.780
Yeah, we'll get to it.

832
00:33:52.900 --> 00:33:56.800
But this is everything that went into, like, the discussion of this storyline.

833
00:33:57.080 --> 00:33:57.280
Right.

834
00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:00.520
Speaking of Hide, I have the cutest thing to share with you.

835
00:34:00.640 --> 00:34:01.300
What is it?

836
00:34:01.420 --> 00:34:05.240
So, you know, we just had that eclipse, right?

837
00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:05.640
Yeah.

838
00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:11.080
And I was talking about it in my Insta stories about the path of totality and all of that

839
00:34:11.080 --> 00:34:13.480
because I love that title.

840
00:34:13.660 --> 00:34:15.960
Like, and they said it so many times, the path of totality.

841
00:34:16.220 --> 00:34:17.380
Are you in the path of totality?

842
00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:18.720
I was like, this should be a movie.

843
00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:19.320
Right?

844
00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:19.540
Right?

845
00:34:19.700 --> 00:34:22.240
This should be a rom-com called The Path of Totality.

846
00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:25.180
But anyway, I was going on and on about it.

847
00:34:25.320 --> 00:34:28.460
And then a few people sent me pictures they took of the eclipse.

848
00:34:28.660 --> 00:34:31.860
And Hide sent me a video that he took of the eclipse.

849
00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:32.200
What?

850
00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:32.700
Yeah.

851
00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:34.520
And I was like, Hide, this is amazing.

852
00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:35.660
And he was like, thank you.

853
00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:38.440
Yeah, but he wasn't in the path of totality, right?

854
00:34:38.620 --> 00:34:39.500
It was a partial.

855
00:34:39.740 --> 00:34:40.620
He was in the partial.

856
00:34:40.860 --> 00:34:42.360
Here in California, we got partial.

857
00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:44.180
But he still sent me his video of it.

858
00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:45.280
Look, here it is.

859
00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:48.080
Oh, it's really good.

860
00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:49.000
It's cool, isn't it?

861
00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:50.680
I don't even understand how he got that.

862
00:34:51.140 --> 00:34:51.660
All right.

863
00:34:51.740 --> 00:34:56.000
Well, next up, Angela enters simply to announce that she'll be leaving.

864
00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:56.720
Yes.

865
00:34:56.840 --> 00:34:59.120
Basically, you've come to work to tell us you're leaving early.

866
00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:00.140
You're so proud.

867
00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:01.100
I'm so proud.

868
00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:02.180
I'm so dressed up.

869
00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:04.000
I'm ready for a very fancy day.

870
00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:08.460
Real quick, at 1 minute 53 seconds, we have a new Meredith wig.

871
00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:12.020
It is a dirty blonde, I would say a mullet.

872
00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:13.400
I think this is a mullet.

873
00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:15.020
It is mullet-like, yes.

874
00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:16.020
Yes.

875
00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:20.320
Angela is going to go on to tell everyone that it's Phillip's first birthday,

876
00:35:20.320 --> 00:35:22.440
and they're hosting a party at their house.

877
00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:25.500
Aaron says, oh, cute, so there'll be a bunch of kids.

878
00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:28.220
And Angela says, oh, no, no children.

879
00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:29.940
Our house is not kid-friendly.

880
00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:31.940
Most of our furniture is sharp.

881
00:35:32.140 --> 00:35:34.760
Also, ew, it will be mostly campaign donors.

882
00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:36.060
Ange?

883
00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:36.660
Mm-hmm.

884
00:35:37.180 --> 00:35:39.200
We got a lot of fan mail about your hair.

885
00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:40.220
Oh, really?

886
00:35:40.820 --> 00:35:42.300
Particularly from Hannah H.

887
00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:43.980
in Cleveland, Ohio, who said,

888
00:35:44.260 --> 00:35:47.920
Angela, your hair is drop-dead gorgeous in this episode.

889
00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:48.420
Hannah!

890
00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:51.900
Would love to hear how this hairstyle was decided upon.

891
00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:53.940
Oh, my gosh, Hannah, you just made my day.

892
00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:55.500
Drop-dead gorgeous.

893
00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:58.000
You know, this was so fun for Kim and I.

894
00:35:58.180 --> 00:35:59.960
Kim Ferry, who did my hair on the show,

895
00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:02.760
we knew we wanted a half-up, half-down,

896
00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:05.160
but she wanted to do something fancy.

897
00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:06.800
See, Kim is phenomenal with hair.

898
00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:10.180
And so she was like, what about a little swirly thing in the back,

899
00:36:10.280 --> 00:36:11.160
and then we'll flip it up.

900
00:36:11.340 --> 00:36:12.760
This was all Kim's vision.

901
00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:14.440
She had a total vision for it.

902
00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:16.560
I said, just go for it, Kim.

903
00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:17.260
I'm here.

904
00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:18.360
I am your canvas.

905
00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:21.000
And then I got to go have a costume fitting,

906
00:36:21.160 --> 00:36:22.320
and they paired it with this dress,

907
00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:23.440
and it just all came together.

908
00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:25.980
You know, I always felt bad for Kim Ferry,

909
00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:28.340
because she was forced to do this just

910
00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:33.060
kind of boring, everyday hair on all of us.

911
00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:33.600
Oh, yeah.

912
00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:34.440
All the time.

913
00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:36.120
Nothing ever fancy.

914
00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:37.680
And so when it came along.

915
00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:38.640
Oh, my gosh.

916
00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:42.660
And because also, she is so good at the fancy hair.

917
00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:43.080
Yeah.

918
00:36:43.240 --> 00:36:44.860
She is so good at an updo.

919
00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:46.640
She is so good at event hair.

920
00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:47.340
Yes.

921
00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:50.360
Like, if you need someone to zhuzh you for your wedding,

922
00:36:50.580 --> 00:36:51.260
she's your gal.

923
00:36:51.260 --> 00:36:52.460
Or a red carpet.

924
00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:56.760
Like, oh, so for her to just have to do low ponies every week.

925
00:36:56.940 --> 00:36:57.600
I know.

926
00:36:57.720 --> 00:36:58.800
Or just a flat iron.

927
00:36:58.980 --> 00:36:59.420
Exactly.

928
00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:00.300
So, yeah.

929
00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:01.720
That was a lot of fun, Hannah.

930
00:37:01.880 --> 00:37:02.340
Thank you.

931
00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:03.660
Well, guess what?

932
00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:05.580
Oscar has a talking head,

933
00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:09.360
and he's going to reveal that he will be attending this party.

934
00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:10.580
Yeah.

935
00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:14.200
Oscar says, Angela's husband and I are in love.

936
00:37:14.320 --> 00:37:15.920
But as a politician in this town,

937
00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:18.980
you still need the conventional blonde wife on your lawn signs.

938
00:37:18.980 --> 00:37:21.280
And then he's very proud.

939
00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:24.260
He goes, he is risking everything to have me there today.

940
00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:25.420
Me.

941
00:37:26.040 --> 00:37:27.780
Well, Angela's going to call Robert.

942
00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:28.700
Guess where she's at?

943
00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:30.320
The sneaky sneak stairway?

944
00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:31.900
The secret sneaky sneak stairwell.

945
00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:32.340
Mm-hmm.

946
00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:35.900
She says, Robert, we agreed that you wouldn't be seen with him in public.

947
00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:37.340
It's humiliating for me.

948
00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:37.880
You know what?

949
00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:40.420
If you get to bring a stud, maybe I do too.

950
00:37:40.980 --> 00:37:43.200
Next, we're going to go to Jim and Daryl's apartment.

951
00:37:43.860 --> 00:37:46.020
Jim is eating some cereal in the kitchen.

952
00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:48.740
He's just, like, leaning against the counter.

953
00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:50.200
He's not sitting at a table.

954
00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:51.960
Daryl's going to enter.

955
00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:55.100
And they have a joint talking head where they say,

956
00:37:55.260 --> 00:37:56.760
we're sharing a sublet in Philly.

957
00:37:57.540 --> 00:37:57.980
Yeah.

958
00:37:58.120 --> 00:37:59.940
They're grown, sexy-ass roommates.

959
00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:03.640
And Jim says, you know, I miss Pam and the kids,

960
00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:06.860
but I'm really enjoying letting my hair down and living the single life.

961
00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:07.980
Excuse me.

962
00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:08.920
You're not single, Jim.

963
00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:10.260
Oh.

964
00:38:10.600 --> 00:38:11.540
You know what, though?

965
00:38:11.540 --> 00:38:12.540
Pam's the bad guy.

966
00:38:13.580 --> 00:38:14.660
So, yeah.

967
00:38:14.660 --> 00:38:14.740
Yeah.

968
00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:19.540
We also find out that Daryl is the clean one and Jim is the messy one.

969
00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:20.660
Yeah.

970
00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:23.240
We had a fan question from Clara D.

971
00:38:23.360 --> 00:38:24.740
in Germany who asked,

972
00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:28.660
I think the storyline of Jim and Daryl sharing the apartment is so funny and relatable.

973
00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:30.380
Do you know who came up with it?

974
00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:33.400
Well, you know, of course, we had to ask the writer, Owen.

975
00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:35.280
And here's what he had to say.

976
00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:38.240
He said, I think I may have technically pitched it,

977
00:38:38.420 --> 00:38:40.180
but really the storyline pitched itself.

978
00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:42.600
Two of our people are in a different city.

979
00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:44.520
They'd probably be crashing together.

980
00:38:44.520 --> 00:38:45.980
And what would that look like?

981
00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:50.360
Daryl being quietly finicky was always a character quirk that I loved.

982
00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:53.900
And John leaned into being the bad roommate in such a wonderful way.

983
00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:57.180
Well, I agree that this was a genius storyline.

984
00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:00.040
And it generated a lot of fan mail.

985
00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:04.460
We have a lot of fan catches based on just this opening scene.

986
00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:05.420
Here we go.

987
00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:07.340
First off from Caitlin C.

988
00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:08.780
in Tyler, Texas, who said,

989
00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:12.640
I've always found it strange that Jim is so untidy in this episode

990
00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:15.660
because at the very beginning of the show during his barbecue,

991
00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:19.880
we see that he kept his place and his bedroom very neat and organized.

992
00:39:20.380 --> 00:39:25.000
Yeah, but I think my brain could make the leap that he was having a party

993
00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:26.960
so they cleaned up their apartment for the party.

994
00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:27.840
Sure.

995
00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:28.740
Sure.

996
00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:30.740
And a girl he liked was coming over.

997
00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:31.360
Yes.

998
00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:33.440
So there might have been extra effort.

999
00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:34.620
This is good.

1000
00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:38.000
And finally, we had this fan catch from Noah R.

1001
00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:39.160
in Freeport, Illinois,

1002
00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:40.140
who said,

1003
00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:44.640
Okay, ladies, this is a weird one, and I apologize in advance.

1004
00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:45.680
Okay.

1005
00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:47.800
At 3 minutes 27 seconds,

1006
00:39:48.020 --> 00:39:50.340
when Jim and Daryl are explaining their rooming situation,

1007
00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:54.120
the camera shows Jim's clothes spread out all over the floor.

1008
00:39:54.680 --> 00:39:57.340
